    "text": "Half-Close: (normally initiated by the client) > In TCP, one end can stop sending data while still receiving data. This 1s called a half-close. > It can occur when the server needs all the data before processing can begin. > Example: When client sends data to server to be sorted, the server needs to receive all data before sorting can start. Client, after sending all data, can close the connection in the outbound direction and remain open to receive the sorted data. The server outbound direction must remain open. > Client half-closes the connection by sending a FIN segment. > Server accepts half-close by sending ACK segment. > Data transfer from client to server stops. The server, however, can still send data. > When server has sent all processed data, it sends a FIN segment, which 1s acknowledged by client. > After half-closing of the connection, data can travel from the server to client and acknowledgments can travel from the client to the server. The client cannot send any more data to the server.",
